(FUN-05 — a function name is a bare single word or a single-quoted
 multi-word name (LANGUAGE.md:758, restated for the call site at :882).
 Both forms define and both forms call. The manual's OLD (pre-0.4.15)
 wording paired "bare" with "single word" and "single-quoted" with
 "multi-word", but the compiler already accepted a single-word name
 written SINGLE-QUOTED, at the definition and at the call — so the
 manual was narrower than the language. As of 0.4.15 (#110) the manual
 itself says so explicitly ("a single word may be quoted too"); this
 probe's grammar-level finding (definition/call both accept it) predates
 and is unaffected by #110, which fixed a DIFFERENT gap — resolving such
 a name inside a `{...}` format-string slot (see the new row added
 2026-08-29). Nothing here is wrong; the rule to take from it is that a
 leaf must not treat "single-word names are never quoted" as a rule,
 because no rule says it — except for a single-CHARACTER name, which
 cannot be quoted at all (LANGUAGE.md:696–699: a one-character quoted
 token is a character literal, not an identifier).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox FUN-05.vox -o p && ./p
